    ],
    "classes_invented": "none; the eight are the criterion's own words and the test parses them from the contract"
  },
  "authority_fit": {
    "verdicts": "Run_Artifacts_Format_LOCKED.md:371: PASS for deterministic match or expected degrade, FAIL for mismatch or unexpected divergence, BLOCKED only when fixture/context/runtime prerequisites are missing. Replay_Verification_Contract_LOCKED.md:58: PASS means all expected values, null reasons, states, lineages, content hashes, manifest and seal assertions match.",
    "null_reason_meaning": "Audit_Hash_and_Reproducibility_Contract_LOCKED.md:78 and Downstream_Consumer_Read_Model_Contract_LOCKED.md:38 (per-field null/reason annotations); Reason_Codes_Registry.md defines REPLAY_FINAL_REASON_CODE_MISMATCH as \"final reason code or reason-code counts differ\" and REPLAY_ARTIFACT_HASH_MISMATCH as \"artifact hash or artifact row-count context differs\", which is why value/hash map to artifact counts and hashes and null-reason to the final reason code and the reason-code counts.",
    "explained_versus_unexplained": "Authority does not define an accepted-mismatch state. What is defined: an expected degrade the fixture declares (PASS) versus a mismatch or unexpected divergence (FAIL); every replay mismatch reason code is HARD; REPLAY_MISMATCH is the code for a mismatch that carries no more specific classification. So \"explained\" is what the fixture declares, and a divergence from it is unexplained. This is a reading of existing text, not new semantics: the implementation already has no accepted-mismatch state and none was added. A stricter alternative (an explained mismatch may still be accepted) would need an owner decision and no current authority supports it.",
    "field_to_class_mapping": "A reviewed mapping from each named class to the comparator fields that carry it; the class names are authority, the field mapping is the reviewed reading, pinned in releaseCriterionClassMap()."
  },
  "why_incomplete_at_entry": {
    "classification": "PROOF_GUARD_GAP (guard/rebind gap); not an implementation defect, not a fixture gap, not an orchestration gap",
    "evidence": "Every named class already had a working comparison in ReplayVerificationService (each of 16 neutering probes turned some existing test red) and the comparator maps every mismatch to a registered HARD reason code. The recorded pair was existence-only: B18ReleaseCandidateCriteriaTest::test_the_exact_publication_mismatch_corpus_is_complete checks that named guards exist, and the unperturbed fixture passes. With each of 16 comparisons neutered, neither bound guard went red once, and B18ReplayComparisonExhaustivenessTest went red only for the two frozen-input comparisons (configuration snapshot hash, event/factor hash). Five comparisons in the named classes were guarded by no test in the five replay test files run (lineage publication_run_id, factor_set_hash, factor_set_id, publication_seal_state, publication_id). The table the corpus rested on asserts only that some mismatch occurs, so a class whose own comparison is removed still reads red while a sibling comparison fires; it covers six classes named by MD-S050-R0029 rather than the eight MD-S002 names; and nothing pinned the class list to the contract.",
